<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nuba Mountains</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nuba Mountains are a range of granite hills in South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kordofan">Kordofan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-sudan">central Sudan</a>, home to many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuba-peoples">Nuba peoples</a> speaking different languages. Their hills long gave refuge from slave raiders. The Muslim kingdom of Taqali arose in the north-east of the range in the early 16th century, and the region was conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and later the Mahdist state in the 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nuba-mountains.jpg" alt="Nuba Mountains" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andreas31 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nuba-berge.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
